Bundled in-tree
---------------

  IBM Plex Sans          SIL OFL 1.1          data/fonts/
  IBM Plex Mono          SIL OFL 1.1          data/fonts/
      Five faces: Sans Regular/Medium/SemiBold, Mono Regular/Medium. Reserved
      font name "Plex"; the full licence travels with them in
      data/fonts/LICENCE.txt and the files are shipped unmodified.

      NOT a build dependency and not linked — they are loaded at run time with
      QFontDatabase::addApplicationFont. They are bundled because design.md §3
      names these two families for the entire interface and §12 requires the
      same rendering on every platform: a font the application merely HOPES the
      system has is a different interface on the machine that lacks it.

      OFL-1.1 permits bundling with software under any licence, including
      GPLv3, provided the font itself stays under OFL and keeps its reserved
      name. Both hold: the files are unmodified and the licence ships with them.

  (Shewchuk predicates.c will also be vendored here; public domain, single
   file, and §9.2 marks it as required rather than optional.)

Deliberately hand-written
-------------------------

Article 2.7 requires a mature cross-platform library wherever one exists. These
are the recorded exceptions, each with its reason.

  kentos_cad/command/task.hpp
      The C++20 coroutine Task<T>. kentoscad.md §7.1 makes writing it the decision:
      "cppcoro (MIT, bakımı yavaş) veya kendi minimal Task<T> tipinizi yazın —
      komut altyapısı için 300 satır yeterlidir ve tam kontrol sağlar.
      Öneri: kendiniz yazın."

  kentos_cad/core/text.hpp — turkish_upper
      Seven cased pairs (i/İ, ı/I, ç, ğ, ö, ş, ü) as an exact table. ICU is the
      right answer for collation, normalisation and search, and casing moves onto
      it when those land; pulling in ~30 MB of ICU for seven pairs is not the
      trade today. The table is exact for its domain and is tested.

  kentos_cad/app/flow_layout.hpp — FlowLayout
      Qt ships no wrapping layout as API. The reflowing layout every Qt
      application needs is published as an example (Qt Widgets "Flow Layout"),
      not as a QFlowLayout class, so there is nothing to link against and no
      third-party package that is more mature than the documented pattern
      itself. ~110 lines implementing QLayout::heightForWidth. Replaced the day
      Qt promotes it to API.

  kentos_cad/core/style_rule.hpp — the style catalogue and rule evaluator
      Article 2.7 was applied and found no candidate. Every mature symbology
      engine ships as part of a whole renderer, not as a library: Mapnik, QGIS
      and MapLibre all bind their style model to their own geometry store and
      draw pipeline, and KentOSCad's style column is resolved at COMMIT time into
      an interned StyleId rather than evaluated per frame, which is the opposite
      of what those engines are built to do. What is NOT hand-written any more:
      the symbol layer model itself now follows QGIS's — typed layers with
      per-property units, stacked and drawn back to front — because that model is
      the right one and was reached by the same reasoning twice. QGIS is NOT
      refused on licence:
      it is GPL-2.0-or-later, which is GPLv3-compatible; an earlier revision of
      this file said otherwise and was wrong. The objection is architectural and
      is stated above. OGC SLD/SE 1.1 is a
      wire format, not an engine, and is planned as an import/export boundary
      behind KENTOS_WITH_LIBXML2, converted to this table on the way in.
      What is NOT hand-written: JSON parsing (kentos_cad/core/json.hpp, itself
      slated for replacement by the pinned library), and the hatch clipping the
      renderer will need, which is Clipper2's job when the fill path lands.

  tests/support/kentos_test.hpp — PENDING and FAIL_WITH
      Two macros on top of doctest, not a framework. PENDING marks a case that
      CANNOT RUN because an optional dependency is off, which .claude/data.md
      requires to report as pending and never as passing; doctest's own skip is
      a compile-time decorator and cannot express a fact this binary discovers
      at run time. FAIL_WITH puts a failure's two halves on two lines.

      This entry used to name microtest.hpp and core/log.hpp as stand-ins for
      doctest and spdlog. Both stand-ins are gone: logging is spdlog behind
      kentos_cad/command/log.hpp, and the tests are doctest.

  kentos_cad/core/trig.hpp — sin_cos_udeg
      Sine and cosine over micro-degrees. Article 2.7 was applied and found no
      candidate: IEEE-754 does not specify the transcendentals, every libm
      implements them differently, and two conforming platforms may disagree by
      an ulp — which is invisible on screen and fatal to a golden fixture that
      §7.3 requires to be bit-identical across three operating systems.

      This is not a claim of being better than libm; libm is faster and at least
      as accurate. It is a claim of REPRODUCIBILITY: the file uses nothing but
      the four operations IEEE-754 does specify exactly, with -ffp-contract=off
      already mandated by CLAUDE.md 2.5.

      crlibm and core-math are correctly rounded and would replace this outright.
      Neither is packaged for Windows, macOS and Linux today, which is the
      cross-platform half of Article 2.7's test; when one is, this file is
      deleted rather than kept. Accuracy is measured against glibc by a test
      rather than asserted in a comment — the first two versions of it were wrong
      and both claimed not to be.

  the command bus, registry, parser, journal, document model
      No library provides these. They are the product.

